; A bounded pool of concurrent tasks, joined in SUBMIT order, built on
; the primitives `(parallel ...)` desugars to.
;
; This replaces `stdlib/Job.ax`, which was a pool of child PROGRAMS over
; `sysSpawn`/`sysWaitPid`. The replacement is strictly more general:
; `parRunAll` is `parMapWords` over a closure that runs one argv, and
; `parMapWords` will run any Axiom thunk. `docs/memory-model.md`
; MM-PAR-2 named "`Job` cannot run an Axiom closure - it execs an
; external program" as Job's hard limit; that limit is lifted here.
;
; WHY `__proc_spawn` AND NOT `__par_spawn`, and why that is not a
; concession to AX3064.
;
; `parMapWords` hands the caller's thunk to a spawn, and that thunk
; captures whatever the caller wrote - a `Vec` of argv vectors, in
; `parRunAll`'s case. AX3064 (`capSpawnHead`, self_host/typecheck.ax)
; refuses a captured reference at `__par_spawn` and `__thread_spawn`,
; because those two may lower to a pthread SHARING the parent's address
; space, where a non-atomic refcount is a race (MM-PAR-6).
;
; `__proc_spawn` NAMES the forked lowering. Its isolation is MM-PAR-3's
; by construction - the child gets a copy-on-write address space, so a
; captured reference is the child's own - and `capSpawnHead`'s own
; header says the exemption is a statement about that primitive rather
; than about a build flag. So this module does not weaken, dodge or
; argue with the rule: it names the lowering whose guarantee it needs.
;
; AND AX3064 CANNOT CHECK THIS MODULE'S CALLERS, which is the part a
; reader has to be told rather than left to assume.
;
; `checkSpawnCaptures` inspects argument 0 of a spawn only when it is
; LITERALLY a lambda; anything else - a parameter, a `let`-bound name -
; is a symbol rather than a record and the walk answers 0. `parMapWords`
; spawns `f`, a PARAMETER, so the check never sees the thunk a caller
; wrote. Measured on this compiler, 2026-09-03: a literal
; `(__par_spawn (lambda (w) (+ (strLen s) w)) 1)` capturing a `String`
; draws AX3064, and the same lambda handed to a one-line wrapper
; `(fn (viaHop f w) (__par_join (__par_spawn f w)))` draws nothing and
; runs. That hole is not opened by this module and not closed by it -
; it is `__par_spawn`'s, and it is reported separately.
;
; What it means HERE is that the safety of every caller's capture rests
; on ONE fact: this file names `__proc_spawn`. Nothing in the type
; system will notice if that word changes. So the fact is GATED rather
; than commented - `scripts/check-parallel.sh` section 6 arm (b) builds
; this pool with `--threads` and without and requires the two modules to
; be BYTE-IDENTICAL, which they are (measured: 0 pthread declares and 0
; `thread_local` lines out of both). Ablated - `__proc_spawn` swapped
; for `__par_spawn` in a copy of `stdlib/` - the `--threads` build grows
; 2 pthread declares and 7 thread-local globals and the arm goes red.
; That gate is standing in for the check the rule cannot make.
;
; WHY THE RESULT ORDER IS THE SUBMIT ORDER, ALWAYS. Inherited from
; `Job.ax` unchanged, for the reason it gave: a pool that answered in
; completion order is a pool whose output depends on which core was
; free, and this project compares bytes for a living. `__axiom_par_join_proc`
; is a `wait4` on a SPECIFIC pid, not a `wait` for any child, which is
; what lets "join the oldest outstanding" bound the width without
; reordering anything. Completion order is not exposed; there is
; deliberately no API for it. That is MM-PAR-5.
;
; WIDTH BOUNDS RUNNING TASKS, NOT SUBMISSIONS. The submit loop blocks
; by joining the oldest outstanding handle whenever `width` are already
; out, so a thousand-case sweep never forks a thousand processes. The
; width is CLAMPED to at least 1 rather than trusted: a width of 0
; would never spawn and never finish.
;
; THE ONE BEHAVIOUR THAT DIFFERS FROM `Job`, and a caller must know it.
; A thunk that TRAPS kills the PARENT. `__axiom_par_join_proc` re-raises
; a non-zero wait status as the parent's own exit - that is
; `tests/stdlib/471-parallel-trap.ax`'s pinned rule (status 77) and it
; is not this module's to change. Measured: three thunks whose middle
; one indexes a `Vec` out of range prints the first answer, then exits
; 77 with the trap's backtrace, and the remaining handles are never
; joined.
;
; This does NOT affect `parRunAll`'s contract. An external program that
; fails - exits 42, dies of a signal, or does not exist at all - is not
; a trap: `parRunOne` answers a word and its child exits 0. Only an
; Axiom-side trap inside the thunk propagates, and a caller wanting
; per-slot failure of those cannot have it without changing the join.
;
; THE ERROR CONTRACT IS `sysRun`'S, EXACTLY, unchanged from `Job`:
;
;   >= 0   the child's exit code
;   128+n  the child was killed by signal n
;   < 0    the spawn's own -errno: the program NEVER RAN
;
; That last distinction is the one a driver must not lose - "could not
; start llc" and "llc rejected the module" are different failures.
(import Mem)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

(import Sys)

(import Err)

; Run `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once,
; answering the results in SUBMIT order.
;
; `hs` holds one handle per spawn and `out` one word per join, and the
; two are read in lockstep by index: `joined` is how far `out` has got
; through `hs`. They are NOT two parallel vectors pushed together - that
; is the discipline `Job.ax` had to keep by hand between `pids` and
; `codes`, and the shape `codegen.ax`'s `bares`/`fulls` pair got wrong
; the hard way. Here `out`'s length IS `joined`, so the invariant is a
; consequence of the loop rather than something to maintain.
(pub :: parMapWords (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int (Vec Int)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (parMapWords f n width)
  (let (
    (w (if (< width 1)
      1
      width
    ))
    (hs vecNew)
    (out vecNew)
    (mut i 0)
    (mut joined 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          ; Block before spawning, not after, so at most `w` children
          ; are ever outstanding. `(- i joined)` is the count of
          ; handles spawned but not yet joined.
          (while (>= (- i joined) w)
            {
              (vecPush out (__proc_join (vecGet hs joined)))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          (vecPush hs (__proc_spawn f i))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      ; The drain. It joins the OLDEST outstanding first, which is what
      ; keeps `out` in submit order; joining the newest would answer the
      ; same multiset in a different order, and `check-parallel.sh`
      ; ablates exactly that.
      (while (< joined (vecLen hs))
        {
          (vecPush out (__proc_join (vecGet hs joined)))
          (set joined (+ joined 1))
        })
      out
    }
  )
)

; A NULL-terminated array of char* from a Vec of `String`, which is the
; shape `execve` and `posix_spawn` both take.
;
; Every element goes through `strDup`, and that is not defensive
; copying - it is required. `strCStr` is `strData` and nothing more
; (Str.ax), so it is NUL-terminated only because every `String` that
; module BUILDS is. A `strSlice` is not built, it is `strWrap` over
; somebody else's buffer, so its bytes run on into whatever follows and
; the kernel reads a path or an argument that does not end where the
; `String` does. `tests/stdlib/300-process.ax` records the same rule for
; the same reason on its own argv helper, and
; `tests/stdlib/476-par-pool.ax` term 2 is the case that catches it.
(pub :: parArgvVector (-> (Vec String) Int))

(pub fn (parArgvVector argv)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen argv))
    (mut i 0)
  )
    (let ((v (memAlloc (* 8 (+ n 1)))))
      {
        (while (< i n)
          {
            (memSetWord v i (strCStr (strDup (vecGetStr argv i))))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        (memSetWord v n 0)
        v
      }
    )
  )
)

; Run one argv - element 0 is the program, looked up on `PATH` the way
; `sysRunPath` does it.
;
; THIS ANSWERS A `Result` AND NOT A SENTINEL, and `Job.jobSubmitArgv`
; did not. That is not a gratuitous difference: `sysRunPath` has
; answered `(Result Int Error)` since 0.3.4, `Job` immediately re-encoded
; it back into an Int, and `compat/SENTINELS` counts exactly that kind of
; re-encoding on a PUBLIC name. The word form is still needed - only a
; word crosses a join - but it is needed in ONE place, so it lives there
; and is private (`parRunWord` below) rather than being this module's
; public surface.
;
; An EMPTY argv is `Err ENOENT` without spawning anything. There is no
; program to run, and "the program never ran" is exactly what that
; means.
(pub :: parRunOne (-> (Vec String) (Result Int Error)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (parRunOne argv)
  (if (== (vecLen argv) 0)
    (Err (mkError 2 "parRunOne: an empty argv names no program"))
    (sysRunPath (strDup (vecGetStr argv 0)) (parArgvVector argv) sysEnvp)
  )
)

; `parRunOne` collapsed into the one word a join can carry, which is
; where `sysRun`'s sign convention gets re-established:
;
;   >= 0   the child's exit code
;   128+n  the child was killed by signal n
;   < 0    the program NEVER RAN, as a negated errno
;
; MATCH, not `unwrapOr`. The sign convention carries "the program never
; ran" in the same Int as an exit code, so a mechanical unwrap would
; destroy the distinction without the checker noticing; the arms cannot.
; That is the argument `Job.ax` made after `302-job` HUNG rather than
; failed, and it is inherited here rather than rediscovered.
;
; PRIVATE, deliberately. A caller who wants the failure in the type calls
; `parRunOne`; a caller who wants a pool calls `parRunAll` and reads the
; convention its header states. Nothing needs the lossy form by name.
(:: parRunWord (-> (Vec String) Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (parRunWord argv)
  (match (parRunOne argv)
    ((Ok c) c)
    ((Err e) (- 0 (errCode e)))
  )
)

; Run every command in `cmds` at up to `width` at once, answering their
; exit codes in the order they appear in `cmds`.
;
; `cmds` is a Vec of Vecs: each inner Vec is an argv whose element 0 is
; the program, exactly as `sysRunPath` expects. This is the shape a gate
; script wants - a list of cases, a width, and an answer per case in the
; order it was listed - and it is `Job.jobRunAll`'s signature and
; contract unchanged, which is what makes the replacement a drop-in.
;
; The lookup of element 0 happens in the CHILD here, where `Job` did it
; in the parent. The observable contract is the same either way: a
; missing program answers `-ENOENT` in its own slot and occupies that
; slot, because `sysRunPath` resolves the name BEFORE it spawns and
; answers `Err` without ever exec'ing. What changed is only which
; process pays for the `PATH` walk.
(pub :: parRunAll (-> (Vec (Vec String)) Int (Vec Int)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (parRunAll cmds width)
  (parMapWords
    (lambda (i) (parRunWord (vecGet cmds i)))    (vecLen cmds)    width
  )
)
